  <!-- Article Body -->
  <section class="bg-[#F8F8F5] py-16">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 shadow-sm p-8 md:p-12 prose prose-lg max-w-none">

        <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Moving into a new home is exciting — but in the middle of unpacking boxes and changing your address, it's easy to overlook one of the most important steps you can take for your family's security. The moment you get those keys from the previous owner or real estate agent, there are almost certainly other copies of those exact keys floating around that you don't know about. Here's why rekeying or replacing your locks should be at the very top of your move-in to-do list.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Who Might Have a Key to Your "New" Home?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Think about all the people who could have had a key to your home before you moved in:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Previous owners (and their family members)</li>
          <li>Real estate agents and buyer's agents</li>
          <li>Contractors who worked on the property</li>
          <li>House cleaners or staging crews</li>
          <li>Neighbors who held a spare key</li>
          <li>Previous tenants (if it was ever a rental)</li>
          <li>Property managers or listing agents</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Most of these people are perfectly trustworthy. But even if they are, you have no way of knowing whether their copy was ever lost, whether they made additional copies, or whether anyone they know had access to it. The only way to be certain your new home's locks are truly yours is to change who can open them.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Rekeying vs. Replacing — What's the Right Choice for a New Home?</h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Rekeying: The Fast, Affordable Option</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Rekeying changes the internal pin configuration of your existing lock cylinder so that all previous keys stop working and only your new key operates the lock. The hardware stays the same — you're just resetting which key it recognizes. This is the most cost-effective move-in security step for most Dover homeowners.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Our <a href="/services/lock-rekeying/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">lock rekeying service</a> can cover all exterior door locks in a typical home in a single appointment, often in under an hour. We can also rekey everything to a single key — so your front door, back door, and side entry all work from the same key — which is a convenience most homeowners appreciate.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          <strong>Rekeying is the right choice when:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>The existing locks are in good working condition</li>
          <li>You're happy with the lock brands already installed</li>
          <li>Budget is a primary consideration</li>
          <li>You want the fastest solution</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Lock Replacement: The Upgrade Option</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If the existing locks are old, worn, low-grade, or you want to upgrade to higher-security hardware, move-in day is the perfect time to make the switch. Many older homes in Dover still have basic Grade 3 locks that offer minimal security. Installing ANSI Grade 1 deadbolts on all exterior doors dramatically improves your home's resistance to kick-in and picking attempts.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Our <a href="/services/deadbolt-installation/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">deadbolt installation service</a> and <a href="/services/lock-change/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">lock replacement service</a> can upgrade your entire home's exterior hardware in a single visit.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          <strong>Lock replacement is the right choice when:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>The existing locks are worn, stiff, or have visible damage</li>
          <li>The locks are more than 10–15 years old</li>
          <li>You want to upgrade to a higher-security grade</li>
          <li>You want to add smart lock or keypad functionality</li>
          <li>You want all locks keyed to a single new key with fresh hardware</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Don't Forget These Areas Beyond the Front Door</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Most homeowners focus on the front door, but every entry point to your home should be secured:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Back door and side entries</strong> — Often overlooked but just as important as the front door.</li>
          <li><strong>Garage entry door</strong> — The door connecting your garage to the interior of your home should have a quality deadbolt.</li>
          <li><strong>Sliding glass doors</strong> — These need a separate security bar or pin lock in addition to the standard latch.</li>
          <li><strong>Basement doors</strong> — Often have older or weaker locks; check these carefully.</li>
          <li><strong>Mailbox</strong> — If your home has a lockable mailbox, consider replacing that as well. We provide <a href="/services/mailbox-lock-replacement/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">mailbox lock replacement</a> for this exact situation.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">What About New Construction Homes?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Even brand-new homes need attention. During construction, builders use temporary "construction locks" — generic, non-keyed cylinders — that are often swapped for permanent locks before closing. But the permanent locks installed by builders are almost always basic Grade 2 or Grade 3 hardware, and multiple construction crew members typically have access to the keys during the build.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Rekeying or upgrading locks in new construction is just as important as in a resale home. Your builder isn't thinking about post-construction key control — that's your responsibility as the new owner.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">When to Call a Locksmith for Your Move-In</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Ideally, schedule a locksmith appointment for move-in day itself — or within the first day or two in your new home. At 1st Capital Locksmith, we work with homeowners throughout Dover, Smyrna, Milford, Harrington, and all of Kent County, Delaware. We can rekey existing locks, install new deadbolts, key everything to a single master key, or do a full security assessment of your new property.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Call us before or on move-in day to schedule your appointment. We offer flexible availability to fit around your moving schedule.
        </p>

